var PEDHD_HAEM_DRAFT_B = [

{
  id:'pedhd-haem-14', bank:'house', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'haematology',
  stem:'Lizzie is a 9-year-old girl who presents to the paediatric clinic. She is known to have hereditary spherocytosis. Her mother is concerned that she is very pale. Three weeks ago Lizzie had an upper respiratory tract infection. Her mother reports that she had a fever and was very flushed with bright red cheeks. She is otherwise well and has fully recovered from her infection. Her mother informs you that she has a very good diet. A full blood count reveals a Hb 88 g/L. A blood film reveals a normochromic normocytic anaemia with no blast cells. Which of the following is the best treatment option for this patient?',
  options:['Vitamin K','Blood transfusion','Folic acid supplementation','Recombinant factor IIIV','No action required at present'],
  answer:4,
  explanation:'**“A fever and bright red cheeks” three weeks ago is parvovirus B19, which arrested her marrow on top of hereditary spherocytosis. The deciding word is *transient* — she has recovered, and a well child at 88 g/L needs watching, not treating.**\n\n**The rash names the virus.** `infectious diseases causing maculopapular rash.pdf` describes erythema infectiosum in two phases: a prodrome of *“low-grade fever, headache, malaise, and mild upper respiratory symptoms (coryza)”*, then **Stage 1 (“Slapped-Cheek”)** — *“bright red, erythematous flushing on the cheeks with circumoral pallor”*. That is the mother’s description word for word, and the only detail explaining the anaemia.\n\n**Why the combination is dangerous.** The same file gives the mechanism — B19 *“specifically targets and replicates in erythroid progenitor cells (immature red blood cells) in the bone marrow”* — and the complication: *“**Aplastic Crisis:** In children with high red cell turnover (e.g., Sickle Cell Disease or Thalassemia), the virus can temporarily shut down red blood cell production, leading to life-threatening anemia”*. Spherocytosis is such a state: `8) COMMON PROBLEMS IN THE TERM NEWBORN.pdf` calls it *“the most common of the red cell membrane defects”*, causing *“hemolysis by decreasing red cell deformability”*. That marrow is already at full stretch; take it offline for a week and the haemoglobin falls fast. *(The lecture’s at-risk list stops at sickle cell disease and thalassaemia — any chronic haemolytic anaemia behaves the same way; not taken from the course material.)*\n\n**Why nothing needs doing.** The arrest is self-limited, about 7–10 days *(the lecture says “temporary” and gives no duration — not taken from the course material)*, and Lizzie is **three weeks out and recovered**. The film agrees — normochromic, normocytic, **no blast cells**. And 88 g/L in an asymptomatic child is far above any threshold: `Iron deficiency anemia pdf.pdf` reserves transfusion for *“severely anemic children with hemoglobin level below 4 g/dl and signs of cardiac dysfunction”*.\n\n**Why the others are wrong**\n\n| Option | Why it does not fit |\n|---|---|\n| Vitamin K | Treats a coagulopathy with a prolonged prothrombin time. Lizzie has no bleeding and no clotting abnormality — the wrong system entirely |\n| Blood transfusion | The genuine second-best: the rash lecture says *“for those in aplastic crisis, blood transfusions or intravenous immunoglobulin (IVIG) may be required”* — but that is the child caught **at the trough**, still falling. Lizzie is well |\n| Folic acid supplementation | The best distractor: chronic haemolysis consumes folate, and long-term folate is standard maintenance in spherocytosis *(not taken from the course material)*. But this marrow was **virally arrested**, not folate-starved, and the stem pre-empts it — *“she has a very good diet”* |\n| Recombinant factor IIIV | **Printed exactly so — VIII transposed.** Factor VIII treats haemophilia A, the answer to `pedhd-haem-13`, not this |\n\n**The shared option menu.** `pedhd-haem-13`, this question and `pedhd-haem-15` print the **same five options** with keys D, E and E — a shared menu **pairs** questions, it never merges them. The stems discriminate: a grossly prolonged APTT with two affected brothers (Q13); a normal film after a viral illness in a known haemolytic (this one); a two-month-old whose haemoglobin has drifted down (Q15).\n\n**The clinical bottom line:** check a **reticulocyte count** — it proves the mechanism and shows the marrow has restarted. `44)BM FAILURE.pdf` gives the clues to red cell aplasia as *“↓ Reticulocyte count despite low Hb”*, *“normal bilirubin”*, *“negative Coombs test”* and *“absent RBC precursors on BM exam”*; in a resolving crisis they rebound above normal. `pedhd-haem-19` is the same virus from the other end.\n\nWritten for this bank — pediatric .pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Parvovirus B19 causes a transient red cell aplasia in any child with a chronic haemolytic anaemia, and a well child who has already recovered from the infection needs a reticulocyte count and observation rather than treatment.',
  source:'pediatric .pdf p.37 (Part I, ch.4 Q14; answer printed on p.38)'
},

{
  id:'pedhd-haem-15', bank:'house', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'haematology',
  stem:'Angie is 6 weeks old. She was jaundiced at 24 hours of age, when her haemoglobin (Hb) was checked and was 150 g/L. At 2 months of age she presents with an upper respiratory tract infection. The full blood count is repeated and she has Hb 102 g/L. Which of the following is the best treatment option for this patient?',
  options:['Vitamin K','Blood transfusion','Folic acid supplementation','Recombinant factor IIIV','No action required at present'],
  answer:4,
  explanation:'**Every infant’s haemoglobin falls over the first two months, and by the material’s own age-specific definition Angie is not anaemic. 102 g/L at two months needs no treatment.**\n\n**The definition settles it.** `44)BM FAILURE.pdf` prints anaemia as *“a reduction of the red cell volume (hematocrit) or hemoglobin concentration below the range of normal values **for age**”*, then the thresholds: *“Neonate: Hb <140 g/L · 1 month – 12 months: Hb <100 g/L · 1 year – 12 years: Hb <110 g/L”*. **Angie is 102 g/L — above the line.** The question invites you to react to the *drop* of 48 g/L rather than the *value*.\n\n**Why the fall happens.** The same file’s haemoglobin table shows the switch: **fetal Hb (2α+2γ) 65 % at birth, 2 % by the sixth month**, **HbA1 (2α+2β) 34 % rising to 95 %**. In utero the fetus is hypoxic, red cell mass high; after birth saturation jumps, erythropoietin shuts down, fetal cells age out unreplaced, and the haemoglobin troughs at **6–9 weeks** before rising. *(The nadir is not stated in the cached lectures — 0 hits for “nadir” across all 64 files — not taken from the course material.)* `pedhd-haem-16` asks the same physiology directly.\n\n**Was the neonatal jaundice real?** Probably. `8) COMMON PROBLEMS IN THE TERM NEWBORN.pdf` states *“visible jaundice with a TSB greater than 5 mg/dL **before 24 hours of age** is most commonly a result of significant hemolysis”*, and warns that after ABO incompatibility *“maternal antibodies may persist for several months after birth, [so] the newborn may become progressively more anemic over the first few weeks of life, occasionally to the point of requiring transfusion”*. **Watching is not treating**: at 102 g/L in a well infant nothing is yet indicated.\n\n**⚠️ The stem contradicts itself, and the contradiction is recorded, not corrected.** The book introduces Angie as *“6 weeks old”* then has her present *“at 2 months of age”*; both are transcribed as printed. It does not change the answer — 102 g/L is above the threshold at either age, and both sit inside the physiological trough.\n\n**Why the others are wrong**\n\n| Option | Why it does not fit |\n|---|---|\n| Vitamin K | Prevents and treats **vitamin K deficient bleeding of the newborn**, which presents with *bleeding* and a prolonged prothrombin time. Angie has neither — an anaemia, not a coagulopathy. `pedhd-haem-11` is the question it answers |\n| Blood transfusion | Reserved for severe, symptomatic anaemia. `Iron deficiency anemia pdf.pdf` sets the bar at *“hemoglobin level below 4 g/dl and signs of cardiac dysfunction”*; Angie is at 10.2 g/dL and well |\n| Folic acid supplementation | Folate deficiency causes a **macrocytic, megaloblastic** anaemia. Nothing in the history — no malabsorption, no established haemolysis, no dietary restriction — suggests it |\n| Recombinant factor IIIV | **Printed exactly so — VIII transposed**, as in `pedhd-haem-13` and `pedhd-haem-14`. Factor VIII treats haemophilia A; Angie has no bleeding |\n\n**The shared option menu.** `pedhd-haem-13`, `pedhd-haem-14` and this question print an identical menu with keys D, E and E — **paired, not duplicated**. Q13 is a factor deficiency with an APTT over 100 seconds, Q14 a post-viral aplastic crisis, this one normal infant physiology.\n\n**The clinical bottom line:** repeat the full blood count **with a reticulocyte count** once the infection settles, and expect a rise. A count that keeps falling, or a reticulocytosis with jaundice, means the neonatal haemolysis is still running — Coombs test and film.\n\nWritten for this bank — pediatric .pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'The haemoglobin of a healthy infant falls physiologically to a trough at around two months, and the course material’s own threshold for anaemia between 1 and 12 months is Hb below 100 g/L — so 102 g/L in a well baby is normal for age.',
  source:'pediatric .pdf p.38 (Part I, ch.4 Q15)'
},

{
  id:'pedhd-haem-16', bank:'house', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'haematology',
  stem:'Haemoglobin production differs through fetal life and extra-uterine life and the molecule itself changes with age. Which of the following statements regarding haemoglobin is correct?',
  options:['At birth HbA is predominant','Hb concentration falls after birth until around 7 weeks','Haematopoesis mainly occurs in the liver and spleen at term','The lifespan of a normal red blood cell is 10 days','Fetal haemoglobin has lower affinity for oxygen'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**Only B is true: a healthy infant’s haemoglobin falls after birth to a physiological trough at 6–9 weeks, then rises.** `44)BM FAILURE.pdf` records the consequence, not the mechanism — its age-specific anaemia threshold drops from *“Neonate: Hb <140 g/L”* to *“1 month – 12 months: Hb <100 g/L”* and back to *“1 year – 12 years: Hb <110 g/L”*. That dip **is** the nadir. *(The 7-week figure is not stated in the cached lectures — 0 hits for “nadir” across all 64 files — not taken from the course material.)* `pedhd-haem-15` is the same fact as a vignette.\n\n**Why the others are wrong**\n\n| Option | Why it is false |\n|---|---|\n| At birth HbA is predominant | Inverted. The same file’s table: **fetal Hb (2α+2γ) 65 % at birth → 2 % at 6 months**, **adult HbA1 (2α+2β) 34 % → 95 %**. HbF predominates at birth; HbA takes over across the first half-year |\n| Haematopoesis mainly occurs in the liver and spleen at term | *(printed with that spelling)* Liver and spleen are the **mid-fetal** sites, about the 2nd to 7th month; the marrow takes over from the 6th–7th fetal month and is the site **at term and after**. The lecture’s “Sites of haematopoiesis periods before and after birth” slide is an image whose text layer holds only the caption |\n| The lifespan of a normal red blood cell is 10 days | A normal red cell survives about **120 days**; 10 days is roughly a **platelet**’s. *(Measured across all 64 cached files: “120 day” and “life span” return 0 hits and “lifespan” hits only `14) Puberty.txt`, in an unrelated sense. Not taken from the course material.)* Shortened survival is what defines a haemolytic anaemia |\n| Fetal haemoglobin has lower affinity for oxygen | Inverted — the point of having HbF at all. γ chains bind **2,3-DPG poorly**, so HbF holds oxygen **more** tightly than HbA and strips it from maternal blood across the placenta. *(Measured across all 64 cached files: “2,3-DPG” returns 0 hits, “DPG” hits only the string UDPGT in `8) COMMON PROBLEMS IN THE TERM NEWBORN.txt`, and the single “affinity” hit is a presepsin receptor in `11) Neonatal sepsis.txt` — the corpus prints the haemoglobin switch but never its oxygen physiology. Not taken from the course material.)* |\n\nWritten for this bank — pediatric .pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Fetal haemoglobin predominates at birth and is replaced by HbA over the first six months, and the infant’s haemoglobin concentration falls to a physiological trough at around two months before rising again.',
  source:'pediatric .pdf p.38 (Part I, ch.4 Q16)'
},

{
  id:'pedhd-haem-17', bank:'house', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'haematology',
  stem:'An 11-month-old caucasian boy attends his GP as his mother has been struggling to wean him. He will drink cow\'s milk at regular intervals and eats some baby rice but refuses most other solids. On examination he appears very pale but otherwise well. There is no hepatosplenomegaly. A full blood count (FBC) is arranged in view of the pallor which shows: Hb = 7.8 g/dL, mean corpuscular volume (MCV) = 69 fL. What is the most likely cause of his anaemia?',
  options:['Sickle cell anaemia','Thalassaemia','Iron deficiency anaemia','Vitamin B12 deficiency','Folate deficiency'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**A microcytic anaemia in an 11-month-old living on cow’s milk and refusing solids is iron deficiency — the commonest anaemia in the world, at the age where it is commonest.**\n\n**The age and the diet are the whole question.** `Iron deficiency anemia pdf.pdf` states *“IDA is the most common anemia worldwide”* and *“IDA has two peaks of incidence: 1. Infancy and Toddlerhood (**6–24 months**) 2. Adolescence”*, then names his exact diet as a cause: *“**early cow milk feeding** (low iron content of cow’s milk and blood loss in the stools due to cow’s milk protein allergy)”* and *“low intake of iron-containing foods”*. Of the three infant milks it compares, **cow’s milk is the worst** — *“low iron content and poor bioavailability, so poor source of iron”* — against breast milk’s *“low iron content but 50 % of iron is absorbed”* and formula *“supplemented with adequate amounts of iron”*. `15.2) Infant Feeding.pdf` adds the timing he has missed: complementary feeding *“typically covers the period from 6 to 24 months of age”*, and *“nutritional gaps start to appear from 6 months in exclusively breastfed infants”*.\n\n**The indices fit.** `44)BM FAILURE.pdf` classifies anaemia by MCV and puts **iron deficiency first among the microcytic causes**. An MCV of 69 fL at 11 months is clearly microcytic, and the IDA lecture’s expected picture is *“↓ Hb, ↓ MCV, ↓ MCH, ↑ RDW, normal/low reticulocytes”* with a smear showing *“anisopoikilocytosis, hypochromia microcytosis, pencil cells”*.\n\n**Why the others are wrong**\n\n| Option | Why it does not fit |\n|---|---|\n| Sickle cell anaemia | **Normocytic**, and it declares itself with **pain and haemolysis** — jaundice, hepatosplenomegaly, dactylitis — none of which he has. He is *“otherwise well”* with **no hepatosplenomegaly**. `pedhd-haem-18` is the sickle vignette |\n| Thalassaemia | The one genuine mimic: `Iron deficiency anemia pdf.pdf` lists *“thalassemia trait”* first among the *“other causes of microcytic hypochromic anaemia”*. Excluded here by the **absence of hepatosplenomegaly**, the lack of any ethnic pointer, and above all a **dietary history that already explains the anaemia**. Thalassaemia major at this age would also show faltering growth and jaundice — see `pedhd-haem-20` |\n| Vitamin B12 deficiency | **Macrocytic**. `44)BM FAILURE.pdf` lists B12 under the macrocytic column; an MCV of 69 points the opposite way |\n| Folate deficiency | Also **macrocytic**, on that same list. Neither produces a small red cell |\n\n**The trap in the stem is the word “weaning”**, there to suggest a nutritional deficiency in general terms where the material forces the specific one. Note too that `15.2) Infant Feeding.pdf` uses *“weaning”* strictly — *“weaning means cessation of breastfeeding”* — where the mother means the introduction of solids.\n\n**The clinical bottom line:** **dietary advice plus oral iron**. The lecture gives *“oral iron salts: 3–6 mg/kg/day”* for *“a minimum of 3 months after anaemia has been corrected to replenish stores”*, and orders the diet: *“cow milk should not be offered to children <12 months and should be limited to <500 mL/day in those older than 12 months”*. **The response is the confirmatory test** — it calls a therapeutic trial the *“best diagnostic study”*, expecting *“reticulocytosis after 48–72 hours, peak 5–7th day”* and an *“increase in Hb after 4–30 days (1 g/dl/week)”*. Warn that iron blackens the stool, may constipate, and stains teeth unless given through a straw; give it with vitamin C. **A child who does not respond has the wrong diagnosis** — then look for thalassaemia or occult blood loss.\n\nWritten for this bank — pediatric .pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Iron deficiency is the commonest anaemia of infancy and toddlerhood, driven by cow’s milk and delayed introduction of iron-rich solids, and it produces a microcytic hypochromic picture that responds to oral iron within days.',
  source:'pediatric .pdf p.38 (Part I, ch.4 Q17)'
},

{
  id:'pedhd-haem-18', bank:'house', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'haematology',
  stem:'A 3-year-old Afro-Caribbean girl presents with severe pain in her hands and abdomen. On examination there is mucosal pallor, yellow sclera, generalized abdominal tenderness with hepatosplenomegaly. Bloods were taken including a FBC: Hb=6.1 g/dL, MCV=78 fL, white cell count (WCC)=6.0 X 10⁹/L, platelets=300 X10⁹/L. What is the most likely cause of her anaemia?',
  options:['Thalassaemia','Iron deficiency anaemia','Sickle cell disease','Anaemia of chronic disease','Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**Pain in the hands plus pain in the abdomen, in an Afro-Caribbean toddler who is jaundiced and anaemic with a normal-sized red cell, is a vaso-occlusive crisis of sickle cell disease.** Three things converge; no other option carries all three.\n\n**1. The MCV is normocytic — that alone kills two options.** `44)BM FAILURE.pdf` sorts anaemia by cell size into **microcytic** (*“Iron deficiency, Thalassemia, Lead poisoning, Chronic disease”*), **normocytic** (*“Production, Blood loss, Hemolysis, Ch. disease”*) and **macrocytic**. An MCV of 78 fL puts the answer in the middle column — where **haemolysis** sits. That lecture’s film panel names the two shapes to look for: *“Spherocytes”* and *“**Sickle cells**”*.\n\n**2. There is active haemolysis.** *“Yellow sclera”* with *“mucosal pallor”* and hepatosplenomegaly is the classic triad of chronic haemolysis — bilirubin from the broken cells, organomegaly from clearing them.\n\n**3. The pain distribution is the fingerprint.** `24)Abdominal pain.pdf` lists *“Sickle disease”* among medical causes of acute abdominal pain and prompts in the history for *“Past medical history: Cystic fibrosis or **sickle cell**”*. **Simultaneous hand and abdominal pain** makes it sickle rather than a surgical abdomen: painful swelling of hands and feet in a toddler is **dactylitis**, often the presenting event, and it happens at this age because the small bones still hold marrow *(measured across all 64 cached lecture files: “dactylitis”, “hand-foot”, “hand and foot” and “vaso-occlusive” each return 0 hits — the corpus says “sickle” 9 times across 5 files, always as an item in a causes list, and never describes a crisis. Not taken from the course material)*.\n\n**Why the others are wrong**\n\n| Option | Why it does not fit |\n|---|---|\n| Thalassaemia | Wrong ethnic frame (Mediterranean) and wrong indices: `44)BM FAILURE.pdf` files *“Thalassemia”* under **microcytic**, and thalassaemia major runs an MCV far below 78 fL. It presents with faltering growth over months, **not acute pain** — that vignette is `pedhd-haem-20` |\n| Iron deficiency anaemia | Also **microcytic** there, and it causes no **jaundice, hepatosplenomegaly or pain** — a production failure, not a haemolysis. `pedhd-haem-17` is the iron-deficiency vignette immediately before this one; the pair teaches the small-cell/normal-cell split |\n| Anaemia of chronic disease | Also in the normocytic column (*“Ch. disease”*), so size will not exclude it — **the story does**. It needs a chronic inflammatory or infective illness in the background, gives a **mild** anaemia rather than 6.1 g/dL, and explains neither the jaundice nor the pain |\n| Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency | Haemolytic and jaundicing, but **episodic and triggered** — drugs, fava beans, infection — with a well child between attacks, no bone pain and no chronic hepatosplenomegaly. It is **X-linked recessive**, and `6) Genetically determined disease_.pdf` notes such conditions are *“usually transmitted only by healthy female carriers”*, making a fully affected **girl** less likely. `pedhd-haem-23` is the G6PD vignette, and it prints its trigger |\n\n**The clinical bottom line:** treat the crisis with **analgesia, hydration, oxygen and warmth**, and take any fever seriously — these children are functionally asplenic. `18) febrile child and life threatening infections.pdf` lists *“Postautosplenectomy in SCD”* among its immunodeficiency red flags, and `specific bacterial infection.pdf` records that *“Daily Penicillin is sometimes used for patients without a functioning spleen (asplenia)”*. The long-range danger is the brain: `53) stroke.pdf` names *“Haematological: sickle cell disease”* as an aetiology of childhood stroke and prescribes *“transfusion therapy in sickle cell disease”*. **A sickle child with new neurology is a stroke until proven otherwise.** When this marrow is switched off by a virus, see `pedhd-haem-19` and `pedhd-haem-14`.\n\nWritten for this bank — pediatric .pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Sickle cell disease presents in a toddler of African ancestry with a normocytic haemolytic anaemia, jaundice, hepatosplenomegaly and vaso-occlusive pain in the hands and abdomen.',
  source:'pediatric .pdf p.39 (Part I, ch.4 Q18)'
},

{
  id:'pedhd-haem-19', bank:'house', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'haematology',
  stem:'A 9-year-old girl with known sickle cell disease presents with a 4-day history of fever, coryza and myalgia. Over the past 24 hours she has developed a lacy rash particularly over her hands and feet and has become more lethargic. Her mother reports her looking more pale. A FBC reveals: Hb 5.9 g/dL, WCC 1.0 X 10⁹/L, platelets 50 X10⁹/L. What is the most likely cause for her blood results?',
  options:['Splenic sequestration crisis','Painful crisis','Chest crisis','Parvovirus B19 infection','Pneumococcal sepsis'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**A coryzal prodrome followed by a lacy rash, in a sickle cell child whose counts have fallen across the board, is parvovirus B19 — an aplastic crisis.**\n\n**The rash is diagnostic, the timing exact.** `infectious diseases causing maculopapular rash.pdf` describes erythema infectiosum in two phases. Prodrome: *“A mild, non-specific illness occurs about a week after exposure… Low-grade fever, headache, malaise, and mild upper respiratory symptoms (coryza)”* — her four days of fever, coryza and myalgia. Then the rash, *“2–5 days after the prodrome”*, through *“Stage 1 (‘Slapped-Cheek’)”* to *“**Stage 2 (Lacy/Reticular)**: 1–4 days later, a pink maculopapular rash appears on the trunk and extensor surfaces of the limbs. As it clears centrally, it takes on a **lacy**, marble-like, or reticular appearance.”* The bank prints stage 2 only.\n\n**The virus attacks the cell line she cannot spare.** The same file states B19 *“specifically targets and replicates in **erythroid progenitor cells** (immature red blood cells) in the bone marrow”*, and lists the complication: *“**Aplastic Crisis**: In children with high red cell turnover (e.g., **Sickle Cell Disease** or Thalassemia), the virus can temporarily shut down red blood cell production, leading to **life-threatening anemia**.”* A sickle red cell survives days, so a fortnight of arrested production takes her from steady state to 5.9 g/dL. The **neutropenia (WCC 1.0) and thrombocytopenia (platelets 50)** mark a marrow, not a sickling, event; B19 transiently suppresses the other lineages *(the extra-erythroid cytopenias are not stated in the cached lectures — not taken from the course material)*. **Expect a reticulocyte count near zero** — that one test separates aplastic crisis from every haemolytic or sequestration cause here.\n\n**Why the others are wrong**\n\n- **Splenic sequestration crisis** — also drops the Hb fast, but into a **suddenly, painfully enlarging spleen**, with hypovolaemia and a **high** reticulocyte count, and it is a disease of the **under-fives**; by 9 most sickle spleens have infarcted away — `18) febrile child and life threatening infections.pdf` calls that *“Postautosplenectomy in SCD”*. No abdominal signs here. *(“sequestration” returns 0 hits across all 64 cached lecture files — the corpus names sickle cell disease but describes none of its crises. Not taken from the course material.)*\n- **Painful crisis** — **there is no pain in this stem**, and pain is the definition. Nor does a vaso-occlusive crisis take the white count to 1.0 or platelets to 50 — if anything it **rises**. `pedhd-haem-18` is that vignette.\n- **Chest crisis** — needs chest findings: tachypnoea, hypoxia, chest pain, an infiltrate. She has coryza and lethargy, nothing respiratory; and it spares the other lineages.\n- **Pneumococcal sepsis** — lethal in a functionally asplenic child; `specific bacterial infection.pdf` records *“Daily Penicillin is sometimes used for patients without a functioning spleen (asplenia)”*. But invasive pneumococcal disease is **acutely toxic and short**, not a four-day coryzal prodrome with a rash, and its white count is typically **high**; a WCC of 1.0 would be an ominous late finding in a sicker child.\n\n**The clinical bottom line:** **transfuse.** The lecture: *“For those in aplastic crisis, blood transfusions or intravenous immunoglobulin (IVIG) may be required”*; there is no antiviral — *“Management is supportive.”* She is **no longer infectious once the rash is out** (*“Once the characteristic rash develops, the child is considered no longer infectious”*), but was maximally so during the prodrome — **keep her from pregnant contacts**, the file listing *“Fetal Hydrops”* after second-trimester infection. `pedhd-haem-14` is the same physiology in hereditary spherocytosis. **Chronic haemolysis plus a sudden fall in haemoglobin with a low reticulocyte count is parvovirus B19 until proven otherwise.**\n\nWritten for this bank — pediatric .pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Parvovirus B19 infects erythroid progenitors and causes a transient aplastic crisis in any child with a shortened red cell lifespan, announced by a coryzal prodrome, a lacy rash and a falling haemoglobin with a low reticulocyte count.',
  source:'pediatric .pdf p.39 (Part I, ch.4 Q19)'
},

{
  id:'pedhd-haem-20', bank:'house', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'haematology',
  stem:'An 8-month-old girl with Greek-Cypriot parents is brought to her GP as her parents are worried she is not growing well and is very pale. On examination there is pallor and mildly icteric sclera. She has a distended abdomen with hepatosplenomegaly and there is mild frontal bossing. She was born on the 25th centile and her weight has fallen to below the 2nd centile. Blood tests show a microcytic hypochromic anaemia. What is the likely diagnosis?',
  options:['Sickle cell disease','Hereditary spherocytosis','Thalassaemia major','Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency (G6PD)','Iron deficiency anaemia'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**A Mediterranean infant who declares herself at 8 months with faltering growth, a microcytic anaemia, jaundice, hepatosplenomegaly and bossing of the skull has beta-thalassaemia major.**\n\n**Why 8 months and not birth.** `44)BM FAILURE.pdf` prints the switching table: **fetal Hb (2α+2γ) 65 % at birth, 2 % at 6 months**, **adult HbA1 (2α+2β) 34 % climbing to 95 %**. A child with no working **beta** chains is protected in utero and through early infancy by her **gamma** chains, becoming anaemic only as the switch completes. **That is why beta-thalassaemia major presents between 6 and 24 months, not on day one** — the fact separating it from every other inherited anaemia here. `pedhd-haem-16` tests the same table as pure recall.\n\n**Indices and inheritance are both sourced.** `44)BM FAILURE.pdf` files *“Thalassemia”* in the **microcytic** column after iron deficiency, and `Iron deficiency anemia pdf.pdf` names *“thalassemia trait”* first among *“other causes of microcytic hypochromic anaemia”* — the bank’s own phrase. `6) Genetically determined disease_.pdf` lists *“Thalassemia”* among *“Examples of AR disorders”* and illustrates the pedigree with *“Consanguineous mating”* — two carrier parents, a one-in-four risk, no affected generation above.\n\n**The bossing is the marrow shouting.** Ineffective erythropoiesis drives marrow expansion; the flat skull bones widen into frontal bossing and maxillary overgrowth, and extramedullary haematopoiesis distends liver and spleen. The jaundice is haemolytic. *(Neither the Mediterranean ethnic distribution of beta-thalassaemia nor the skeletal changes are stated in the cached lectures — “frontal bossing” returns hits only in the two rickets files, never in a haematological context, and “Mediterranean” appears exactly once across all 64 files, in the G6PD paragraph of `8) COMMON PROBLEMS IN THE TERM NEWBORN.pdf`. Not taken from the course material.)*\n\n**Why the others are wrong**\n\n| Option | Why it does not fit |\n|---|---|\n| Sickle cell disease | Wrong cell size — **normocytic**, in `44)BM FAILURE.pdf`’s middle column under *“Hemolysis”*, against the stem’s **microcytic hypochromic**. Wrong ethnicity and story: sickle announces itself with **pain**, not months of faltering growth. `pedhd-haem-18` is the sickle vignette two questions earlier |\n| Hereditary spherocytosis | Right on jaundice and splenomegaly, wrong on the red cell. `8) COMMON PROBLEMS IN THE TERM NEWBORN.pdf` calls it a defect of *“the red cell membrane”*; the cells are small in **diameter** but the anaemia is **normocytic** with a **raised** MCHC, not hypochromic. No skeletal change, no growth failure of this degree. See `pedhd-haem-14` |\n| Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency (G6PD) | **Episodic, not chronic.** `8) COMMON PROBLEMS IN THE TERM NEWBORN.pdf` calls it *“the most common red cell enzyme defect causing hemolysis”* — it bites under oxidative stress, leaving the child well between attacks. **X-linked**, with no microcytosis, no bossing, no failure to thrive. `pedhd-haem-23` gives its trigger-driven presentation |\n| Iron deficiency anaemia | **The one real competitor**: the other microcytic hypochromic anaemia, peaking at exactly this age — `Iron deficiency anemia pdf.pdf` gives *“Infancy and Toddlerhood (6–24 months)”*. Excluded by everything around the blood count: **iron deficiency causes no jaundice, no hepatosplenomegaly and no frontal bossing.** Compare `pedhd-haem-17`, an 11-month-old with identical indices and **none** of those signs |\n\n**The clinical bottom line:** confirm with **haemoglobin electrophoresis** *(“electrophoresis”, “HbA2” and “Hb H” each return 0 hits across all 64 cached files — the corpus names thalassaemia but never how it is confirmed; not taken from the course material)*, then **lifelong transfusion plus iron chelation** — the transfusions will otherwise kill her by iron overload. **The trap is to reach for oral iron on the word “microcytic”**: giving iron to a transfused thalassaemic is actively harmful. **Screen the parents**, both obligate carriers, since thalassaemia trait is a lifelong mimic of iron deficiency.\n\nWritten for this bank — pediatric .pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Beta-thalassaemia major declares itself between six and twenty-four months, as fetal haemoglobin is replaced by adult haemoglobin, with a microcytic hypochromic anaemia, jaundice, hepatosplenomegaly, marrow expansion and faltering growth.',
  source:'pediatric .pdf p.39 (Part I, ch.4 Q20)'
},

{
  id:'pedhd-haem-21', bank:'house', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'haem-bleeding',
  stem:'A 14-month-old boy is brought to accident and emergency by his mother with pain and swelling in his right knee. He is unable to weight bear and on examination several large bruises are noted over the lower limbs and arms. He has blood tests, including a FBC and clotting screen, which show: Hb 10.5 g/dL, WCC 11 X 10⁹/L, platelet count 340 X10⁹/L, prothrombin time 13.3 sec, APTT >120 sec. Which is the most likely diagnosis?',
  options:['Vitamin K deficiency','Haemophilia A','Von Willebrand disease','Immune (idiopathic) thrombocytopenic purpura','Non-accidental injury'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**A boy with a haemarthrosis, an isolated grossly prolonged APTT and a normal PT and platelet count has haemophilia A.**\n\n**Read the screen as a pattern, not three numbers.** `47)Hemorrhagic disorders .pdf` gives the triad exactly: *“**PTT prolonged** {N 25- 45 sec}, **PT normal**, **Factor VIII reduced**.”* His PT of **13.3 sec is normal**; his APTT of **>120 sec** is off the scale — a **severe** defect, graded *“severe 0- 1% activity”*. A normal PT with a very long APTT localises the fault to the **intrinsic** pathway, where factor VIII is what commonly fails.\n\n**The picture matches line for line.** The clinical block reads *“**Male** + family history… Infants and children: * **Ecchymosis** * I.C Hge * **Haemarthrosis** * Intramuscular haematomas…”*. He is male, with **ecchymoses** over the limbs and a knee **haemarthrosis**; severe haemophilia surfaces once a child cruises and walks. It is **X-linked recessive**, and `6) Genetically determined disease_.pdf` explains the silent family history — such conditions are *“usually transmitted only by healthy female carriers.”*\n\n**Why the others are wrong**\n\n| Option | Why it does not fit |\n|---|---|\n| Vitamin K deficiency | Excluded by the **normal PT**. `47)Hemorrhagic disorders .pdf`’s comparison table gives it as **PTT prolonged AND PT prolonged**, with bleeding time, thrombin time, platelet count and fibrinogen normal. It hits factors II, VII, IX and X — **VII** is short-lived, so the **PT goes first**. The opposite pattern |\n| Von Willebrand disease | *(printed here with a capital V, where Q7 and Q8 of this chapter print “von Willebrand disease” — the book is inconsistent with itself.)* The lecture’s table settles it: vWD gives *“Bruising, epistaxis”* against haemophilia’s *“**Joint & muscle bleeding**”*; vWD affects *“Males = females”*, haemophilia *“males”*; vWD’s **PTT is “N or ↑”** against haemophilia’s *“↑”*. An APTT beyond 120 seconds is far outside type 1 vWD, *“a mild to moderate quantitative deficiency of vWF (ie, about 20-50 % of normal levels)”* and *“the most common type, accounting for 60–80 % of all VWD patients”*. **Bleeding into a joint points at the factor, not the platelet plug** |\n| Immune (idiopathic) thrombocytopenic purpura | Excluded by the normal **platelet count of 340 X 10⁹/L**. The same lecture defines ITP on the count — *“CBC :- **isolated thrombocytopenia**, normal platelet shape”* — with the **clotting screen normal**, the mirror of his results, and warns *“presence of HSM or bone or joint pain suggests another diagnosis”*. `pedhd-haem-22` is the ITP vignette: platelets 15, clotting normal |\n| Non-accidental injury | **The right instinct, the wrong answer.** Bruising over several sites in a newly ambulant child is a real safeguarding flag, and `39)Hematuria.pdf` states that *“Child abuse must always be suspected”*. But **a coagulopathy this severe explains every finding**, and a clotting screen is what stops a family being wrongly accused. Both ways: exclude a bleeding disorder before attributing injuries to abuse, and remember one does not make abuse impossible. `pedhd-haem-25` is this chapter’s non-accidental injury question, and its features — a history that does not fit, delayed presentation, changing accounts — are **absent** here |\n\n**The clinical bottom line:** treat with **factor VIII replacement**, dosed by site — *“Haemarthrosis 25 unit/kg/12 hr; Hematuria & GIT 50 unit/kg/12 hr; CNS bleeding 75 unit/kg/8 hr.”* Prevention is what **not** to do: *“Avoidance of trauma, **Avoid IM injection**, Avoid surgery, Non-violent exercise, **Avoid aspirin**”*, plus *“Immunization HBV”*. Watch for **inhibitors**, put at *“25 %”* in haemophilia against *“rare”* in vWD. Haemophilia **B** is *“15 % of cases”* with *“Lab. like haemophilia A”* — only a **factor assay** separates them.\n\nWritten for this bank — pediatric .pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Haemophilia A is an X-linked deficiency of factor VIII that produces joint and muscle bleeding with a prolonged APTT, a normal prothrombin time and a normal platelet count.',
  source:'pediatric .pdf p.40 (Part I, ch.4 Q21)'
},

{
  id:'pedhd-haem-22', bank:'house', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'haem-bleeding',
  stem:'A 5-year-old boy attends accident and emergency with a nosebleed that his mother has been unable to stop for the past 45 minutes. He saw his GP for a viral upper respiratory tract infection 1 week ago but is otherwise well. His nosebleed is stopped with pressure but on examination he is noted to have multiple petechiae on his chest, legs and abdomen. Blood tests reveal: Hb 10.4 g/dL, WCC 13 x 10⁹/L, platelet count 15 x 10⁹/L, clotting screen normal. The most likely diagnosis is which of the following?',
  options:['Henoch–Schonlein purpura','Haemophilia B (Christmas disease)','Immune (idiopathic) thrombocytopenic purpura (ITP)','Meningococcal septicaemia','Haemolytic uraemic syndrome (HUS)'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**A well 5-year-old with petechiae, an isolated platelet count of 15 and a normal clotting screen, one week after a viral illness, has immune thrombocytopenic purpura.** `47)Hemorrhagic disorders .pdf` writes this vignette almost verbatim.\n\n**Trigger, age and sex are in the lecture.** Aetiology: *“The cause of ITP remains unknown in most cases, but **recent history of viral infection** EBV, varicella virus, CMV, rubella virus, or hepatitis virus (A, B, or C).”* Presentation: *“**History of preceding viral infection 1-4 weeks before onset** of thrombocytopenia”* — his was one week ago — and *“The classical presentation is **sudden onset of petechiae and purpura in a previously healthy child**.”* Epidemiology: *“Sex :- Males more than females. Age :- Any age **Peak 1-6 years**.”* He is a 5-year-old boy.\n\n**The blood picture is the definition.** The laboratory line: *“CBC :- **isolated thrombocytopenia**, normal platelet shape”* — one line down, all else normal, exactly the bank’s numbers: Hb 10.4, WCC 13, **clotting screen normal**, platelets 15. Bleeding symptoms: *“Epistaxis, Bleeding gums, Easily bruising”*, with *“Very rarely intracranial hemorrhage (ICH)”* the feared event. The same page clears three distractors: *“**Normal otherwise physical examination; presence of HSM or bone or joint pain suggests another diagnosis.**”*\n\n**Why the others are wrong**\n\n| Option | Why it does not fit |\n|---|---|\n| Henoch–Schonlein purpura | *(printed without the umlaut on the o.)* Wrong rash, wrong place, wrong platelets. `47)Hemorrhagic disorders .pdf` describes it as *“a purplish rash, **typically on the lower legs and buttocks**”* — *“Reddish-purple spots that look like bruises”* — with *“Swollen, sore joint… mainly in the knees and ankles”*, *“belly pain, nausea, vomiting and bloody stools”*, *“Kidney involvement”*. **HSP is a vasculitis with a normal platelet count**, its purpura palpable and gravity-dependent, not flat petechiae. `pedhd-renal-19` is the shipped HSP question |\n| Haemophilia B (Christmas disease) | Excluded by the **normal clotting screen and low platelets** — the reverse of a factor deficiency. The lecture puts it at *“15 % of cases”*, *“Lab. like haemophilia A”*: **PTT prolonged, PT normal**, platelets untouched. It bleeds into **joints and muscles**, not skin petechiae, and is **X-linked**, presenting in infancy. `pedhd-haem-21` is the haemophilia vignette — compare the screens |\n| Meningococcal septicaemia | The diagnosis to exclude at the door — but the stem twice says he is **well**: *“otherwise well”*, nosebleed *“is stopped with pressure”*. It brings fever, toxicity, poor perfusion and a **spreading non-blanching purpuric** rash in a child who looks ill, and deranges the **whole** screen through DIC — `47)Hemorrhagic disorders .pdf` sets that out as *“wide spread deposition of fibrin and microthrombi”* with **PT, PTT, TT all prolonged, fibrinogen ↓↓ and FDP positive**. His is normal. `pedhd-inf-1` is the meningococcal sepsis question |\n| Haemolytic uraemic syndrome (HUS) | Also drops the platelets, but never alone: a **triad** — thrombocytopenia, microangiopathic haemolytic anaemia and acute kidney injury, typically after bloody diarrhoea. He has **Hb 10.4**, no fragmented cells, no oliguria or raised creatinine, and a **respiratory**, not enteric, prodrome. `pedhd-renal-8` and `pedhd-renal-22` are the shipped HUS questions |\n\n**The clinical bottom line:** **treat the child, not the number.** First-line: *“**Wait and Watch (irrespective of platelet count)**”*, then corticosteroids, *“IVIg (single dose)”*, *“IV anti-D”*, because *“Management… is based largely on the **severity of bleeding**.”* Most need nothing: *“mostly it’s an acute disease and **resolves spontaneously within 1-6 weeks**.”* A **marrow aspirate is not required** — *“Not recommended in children with typical ITP”* — and **splenectomy must wait**: *“delayed by ≥12 months.”* Tell the family: no contact sport, **no aspirin or NSAIDs**.\n\nWritten for this bank — pediatric .pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Immune thrombocytopenic purpura follows a viral illness by one to four weeks in a well young child and produces an isolated thrombocytopenia with a normal clotting screen and a normal examination.',
  source:'pediatric .pdf p.40 (Part I, ch.4 Q22)'
},

{
  id:'pedhd-haem-23', bank:'house', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'haematology',
  stem:'A 7-year-old Ghanaian boy presented to accident and emergency, 3 days ago, on his return from holiday in West Africa, with high fever and rigors. A diagnosis of malaria was made and he was started on primaquine. His mother is concerned that he has begun to look increasingly jaundiced. On examination there is deep jaundice, and he is looking pale and breathless. A FBC and film are taken which show: Hb 5.5 g/dL, WCC 15 x 10⁹/L, platelet count 200 x 10⁹/L, blood film: red cell fragments and bite cells with Heinz bodies on staining. The most likely underlying diagnosis is which of the following?',
  options:['Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency (G6PD)','Pyruvate kinase deficiency','Sickle cell disease','Beta-thalassaemia','Hereditary spherocytosis'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**An oxidant drug given to a West African boy, followed by brisk haemolysis with bite cells and Heinz bodies, is G6PD deficiency.**\n\n**The lecture names disease, population and mechanism.** `8) COMMON PROBLEMS IN THE TERM NEWBORN.pdf` states G6PD deficiency *“is the **most common red cell enzyme defect causing hemolysis**, especially in infants of **African, Mediterranean, or Asian** descent”* and *“the disorder is **Xlinked**”* — a 7-year-old **boy** of Ghanaian origin sits there. The enzyme keeps glutathione reduced against oxidative stress; without it an oxidant denatures haemoglobin into precipitates the spleen bites out. **Those precipitates are the Heinz bodies, the bitten remnants the bite cells** — the film shows the mechanism *(“Heinz” and “bite cell” each return 0 hits across all 64 cached lecture files — not taken from the course material)*. One trigger is named: `39)Hematuria.pdf` cites **favism** twice — *“Hemoglobinuria: intravascular hemolysis (e.g., HUS, **favism**)”*, *“Urobilinogen: hemolytic anemia (e.g., **favism**)”*. **Primaquine is the other classic offender** *(0 hits for “primaquine” across all 64 files — not taken from the course material)*, with sulphonamides, nitrofurantoin, naphthalene mothballs and **infection itself** — malaria alone could have done it.\n\n**The trap the lecture prints.** *“Since G6PD enzyme activity is **high in reticulocytes**, neonates with a large number of reticulocytes may have **falsely normal enzyme tests**. A low G6PD level should always raise suspicion. **Repeat testing in suspect cases with initially normal results is indicated at 3 months of age.**”* The same applies here: assaying **during** haemolysis, the marrow flooding the blood with young cells, can read normal. **A normal level mid-crisis does not exclude the diagnosis — repeat it weeks later.**\n\n**Why the others are wrong**\n\n| Option | Why it does not fit |\n|---|---|\n| Pyruvate kinase deficiency | Wrong stem. `8) COMMON PROBLEMS IN THE TERM NEWBORN.pdf` lists it beside G6PD — *“Red cell enzyme abnormalities: glucose6phosphate Dehydrogenase deficiency, **pyruvate kinase deficiency**, hexokinase deficiency”* — but it is **glycolytic**, not oxidative: **chronic** non-spherocytic haemolysis from infancy, **autosomal recessive**, not drug-triggered, and **producing neither Heinz bodies nor bite cells** |\n| Sickle cell disease | Right ethnicity, wrong film and story. Sickle gives a **normocytic** anaemia with **pain** and sickle cells — `44)BM FAILURE.pdf` names *“Sickle cells”* on the film — and does not first declare itself at 7 after a drug, nor without prior haemolysis. `pedhd-haem-18` and `pedhd-haem-19` are the sickle questions |\n| Beta-thalassaemia | Wrong tempo. Thalassaemia major is **chronic microcytic hypochromic**, with faltering growth, hepatosplenomegaly and marrow expansion from late infancy — `44)BM FAILURE.pdf` files *“Thalassemia”* under **microcytic**. It does not appear abruptly after a tablet. `pedhd-haem-20` is the thalassaemia vignette |\n| Hereditary spherocytosis | The other inherited haemolysis, closest on film — but the shape is wrong. `8) COMMON PROBLEMS IN THE TERM NEWBORN.pdf` calls it *“the most common of the **red cell membrane** defects”*, causing *“hemolysis by decreasing red cell deformability”*, *“Diagnosis… suspected by peripheral blood smear and **family history**”*. Its cells are **spherocytes**, not bite cells; inheritance is usually **autosomal dominant**, hence a family history; and the course is chronic, with splenomegaly and gallstones, not a drug-triggered crisis. `pedhd-haem-14` is the spherocytosis vignette |\n\n**The clinical bottom line:** **stop the drug**, give fluids, transfuse a breathless child at 5.5 g/dL. The malaria still needs treating — swap to a non-oxidant antimalarial. Then prevention: **a written list of drugs and foods to avoid**, a warning that any febrile illness can precipitate a crisis, and **test mother and siblings** — `8) COMMON PROBLEMS IN THE TERM NEWBORN.pdf` notes *“female heterozygotes are also at increased risk”* through X-inactivation — carrier girls are not spared.\n\nWritten for this bank — pediatric .pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'G6PD deficiency is an X-linked red cell enzyme defect of African, Mediterranean and Asian populations that causes acute drug- or infection-triggered haemolysis with bite cells and Heinz bodies, and its enzyme assay can read falsely normal during the crisis.',
  source:'pediatric .pdf p.40 (Part I, ch.4 Q23)'
},

{
  id:'pedhd-haem-24', bank:'house', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'malignant',
  stem:'A 4-year-old girl is undergoing chemotherapy for acute lymphoblastic leukaemia. She presents 8 days after her last treatment with a fever of 39C. She has a portacath in situ. Her FBC shows a WCC 1.0 x 10⁹/L, neutrophils 0.4 x 10⁹/L, platelets 100 x 10⁹/L, Hb 10 g/dL. What is the most important step in her management?',
  options:['Packed red blood cell transfusion','Platelet transfusion','Administration of G-CSF','Antipyretics','Intravenous antibiotics'],
  answer:4,
  explanation:'**This is febrile neutropenia: broad-spectrum intravenous antibiotics go up now, before any result comes back.** Nothing else on the list changes whether she survives the week.\n\n**Read the two numbers together.** A neutrophil count of **0.4 x 10⁹/L** eight days after chemotherapy is the expected marrow trough; 39C in that state is sepsis until proven otherwise. `48)Leukemias.pdf` lists among the consequences of marrow failure: *“**Neutropenia: causing fever**, ulceration of buccal mucosa and infection”* — that is marrow invasion by blasts, but chemotherapy empties the marrow the same way. `18) febrile child and life threatening infections.pdf` puts her in its highest-risk group under *“Are there risk factors for infection?”* — *“**Increased susceptibility from immunodeficiency**”* — and its management principle is unambiguous: *“**No delay in the administration of antibiotics and supportive therapy.**”* *(Printed in the meningitis section, not under neutropenia; the principle transfers, the citation does not pretend otherwise.)*\n\n**Two things make her more dangerous than an ordinary febrile 4-year-old.** With **no neutrophils** the usual localising signs are muted — no pus, no consolidation, no red throat — and *“Follow-up of the clinical course remains the most sensitive indicator”* is unsafe when deterioration takes four hours. And her **portacath** is a line straight into the central circulation and the commonest portal of entry; culture **the line and a peripheral vein** so a line infection can be identified. *(Measured across all 64 cached lecture files: “portacath”, “port-a-cath” and “indwelling” return 0 hits, and the only central access named is neonatal — *“Central lines including UC, PICC lines”* in `11) Neonatal sepsis.pdf`, listed as a sepsis risk factor, never an oncology device. “Absolute neutrophil”, “febrile neutropen”, “G-CSF”, “GCSF” and “granulocyte” likewise return 0 hits, so no neutrophil threshold is stated anywhere. Not taken from the course material: the working definition is a single temperature ≥38.5C, or ≥38C sustained, with a neutrophil count below 0.5 x 10⁹/L, and antibiotics within one hour, covering Pseudomonas.)*\n\n**Why the others are wrong**\n\n| Option | Why it is not the most important step |\n|---|---|\n| Packed red blood cell transfusion | **Her Hb is 10 g/dL — barely anaemic.** `Iron deficiency anemia pdf.pdf` reserves transfusion for children *“with hemoglobin level below 4 g/dl and signs of cardiac dysfunction or associated with infection and hypoxia”*; oncology thresholds are more generous but nowhere near 10 |\n| Platelet transfusion | **Platelets 100 x 10⁹/L is a normal count**, and no bleeding is described. Prophylactic platelets belong an order of magnitude lower |\n| Administration of G-CSF | Right problem, wrong answer: it treats the **count**, not the **infection**, and takes days while she can die in hours. Growth factors shorten neutropenia in selected high-risk protocols; they are not the response to a fever *(not taken from the course material — see the measurement above)* |\n| Antipyretics | Legitimate comfort care — `18) febrile child and life threatening infections.pdf` allows *“The use of antipyretic agents either paracetamol or ibuprofen can be used”* — but **alone** they are dangerous here, removing the one sign you are monitoring. And a **platelet-safe** choice matters in an oncology patient: paracetamol, not ibuprofen |\n\n**The clinical bottom line:** cultures, then antibiotics **within the hour** — do not wait for the cultures. This is the counterweight to the same lecture’s closing warning, *“**Do not abuse antibiotics: FEVER does not mean ANTIBIOTIC**”*: that rule is for the **immunocompetent** child, and **neutropenia reverses it**. She is its modal patient — `48)Leukemias.pdf` puts *“Acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL)”* at **75 %** of childhood acute leukaemias with *“Peak incidence between 2 and 5 years of age”*. **Teach the parents one sentence: any fever, come straight in.**\n\nWritten for this bank — pediatric .pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Fever in a neutropenic child on chemotherapy is sepsis until proven otherwise and demands blood cultures followed immediately by broad-spectrum intravenous antibiotics, ahead of any transfusion, growth factor or antipyretic.',
  source:'pediatric .pdf p.41 (Part I, ch.4 Q24)'
},

{
  id:'pedhd-haem-25', bank:'house', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'haem-bleeding',
  stem:'A 5-month-old baby with no previous attendances at hospital is brought to the hospital by his distraught parents with a swollen left upper arm. Both parents tell you separately that he was playing on the floor and fell to one side. They noticed he was crying so phoned an ambulance to come to hospital. A radiograph of the arm shows a spiral fracture of his left humerus. Which of the following features suggest non-accidental injury?',
  options:['Immediate presentation at hospital','History of incident does not fit with injury','Parents appearing distraught','Both accounts of the incident matching','No previous attendances at hospital'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The one feature that raises the alarm is that the story does not explain the fracture.** Every other option is a *reassuring* feature printed to be rejected — the question is an inversion, and B the only genuine flag.\n\n**Why the history does not fit.** A **5-month-old** cannot cruise, walk or climb; his stated activity is *“playing on the floor”* and a **fall to one side from sitting or lying**, carrying almost no energy. A **spiral fracture** is **torsional**: the bone twisted along its long axis, requiring the limb to be gripped and rotated. **A low-energy sideways topple cannot generate torque along a humerus.** The mechanism offered and the one required differ, and that mismatch — not the parents’ demeanour, not the timing — is the finding. Any fracture in a **non-ambulant** infant deserves an explanation; a spiral one of a long bone, a very good one. *(The biomechanics of a spiral fracture, and the specific list of features that raise suspicion of abuse, are absent from the cached lectures — “spiral fracture” returns 0 hits across all 64 files, and no lecture carries a safeguarding checklist. Not taken from the course material.)*\n\n**What the material does say about abuse.** Three lectures name it. `39)Hematuria.pdf` states the principle: *“**Child abuse must always be suspected** in the child presenting with unexplained perineal bruising and hematuria.”* `53) stroke.pdf` names the lesion to fear at this age — subdural haemorrhage from *“tearing of the bridging veins as they cross the subdural space”*, which *“is a **characteristic lesion in nonaccidental injury caused by shaking or direct trauma in infants and toddlers**”*, adding *“**Retinal haemorrhages are typical of shaking injury**”*. `58) cerebral palsy.pdf` lists *“intracranial trauma e.g. **child abuse**”* among the acquired causes — the cost of a missed diagnosis. **The material gives the consequences of missing it; the pattern-recognition is yours.**\n\n**Why the others are wrong** — each is the *opposite* of a warning sign:\n\n- **Immediate presentation at hospital** — reassuring. It is **delayed** presentation, or a carer attending only when the injury cannot be hidden. These parents phoned an ambulance as he cried.\n- **Parents appearing distraught** — reassuring, and a trap in both directions. Distress is the expected response. **Demeanour is a poor discriminator**: abusive carers can be distraught, innocent carers flat with shock. Never judge safeguarding on how someone looks.\n- **Both accounts of the incident matching** — reassuring. **Inconsistency** — accounts that differ between carers, or **change** on retelling — is the classic flag. Note the stem’s wording: they were asked *“separately”*, the right technique, and agreed.\n- **No previous attendances at hospital** — reassuring. **Repeated attendances**, injuries at different stages of healing, or a child already known to services are the concern. This is his first.\n\n**The clinical bottom line:** four reassuring features do **not** clear the case: **one unexplained mechanism outweighs them all**. Next steps are procedural: **admit for his own safety**, involve the **named safeguarding doctor** and social care, document the history in the carers’ own words, and complete the workup — **skeletal survey** for occult and healing fractures, **fundoscopy** for the retinal haemorrhages `53) stroke.pdf` flags, **head imaging** on any concern. Do the differential too: **a bleeding disorder must be excluded before injuries are attributed to abuse** — `pedhd-haem-21` is this question’s mirror, where bruising and a swollen joint in a 14-month-old proved to be haemophilia A — and **bone disease matters**: osteogenesis imperfecta and rickets both fracture easily *(not taken from the course material)*. **Neither exclusion is a reason to delay protecting the child.**\n\nWritten for this bank — pediatric .pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'The cardinal indicator of non-accidental injury is a history that cannot account for the injury sustained, particularly a fracture in a non-ambulant infant, and reassuring features such as prompt attendance or consistent parental accounts do not exclude it.',
  source:'pediatric .pdf p.41 (Part I, ch.4 Q25)'
},

];
